<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Austria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> is the largest state of Austria, lying in the north-east of the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, which was long its capital; St. Pölten replaced it in 1986. The region formed the heart of the medieval duchy of Austria under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> occupied much of it, together with Vienna, from 1485 until his death in 1490.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-austria.jpg" alt="Lower Austria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Written by Jon Harald Søby and Gryffindor; colours and ratio adjusted by Mevsfotw. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lower_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Industry grew mainly in <b>Lower Austria</b> and Bohemia, with textiles, glass, iron and sugar, while Hungary remained overwhelmingly agrarian and was separated from the western lands by an internal customs line until 1851.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liechtenstein family, named after Liechtenstein Castle south of Vienna, had become one of the richest noble houses of the Habsburg monarchy, with vast estates in Moravia, Bohemia and <b>Lower Austria</b>, and Karl I of Liechtenstein was raised to the rank of prince in 1608.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Franz von Saurau, President of <b>Lower Austria</b>, took up the idea: as he later wrote, he had a text made by the poet Lorenz Leopold Haschka and turned to Haydn as the one composer who could write something to stand beside the British anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>The Theresian Military Academy was founded in the castle of Wiener Neustadt in <b>Lower Austria</b>, once a residence of Emperor Frederick III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1804 Francis II proclaimed the Austrian Empire, in which the archduchy remained a crown land until 1918; its territory now forms the federal states of <b>Lower Austria</b>, Upper Austria and Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/state/archduchy-of-austria">Archduchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>The federal constitution of 1920, drafted with the help of Hans Kelsen, created a federal parliamentary republic; with Burgenland added in 1921 and Vienna separated from <b>Lower Austria</b> in 1922, it had nine states.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-austrian-republic">First Austrian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
